A trully great game. 9/10
Pros:
- The setting is not post-apocalyptic in any way shape or form. The setting is as real as you can get which makes immersion so much easier;
- You are not member of any military group. To be honest, I had enough with military style machoism, chestbeating, brain washed drone mentaility and overblown "patriotism" (read propaganda) in FPS games. I get enough of that from mass media already;
- There are no Zombies!
- Environment! Really one of the best parts of the game. Have a FPS in a tropical island environment? Why not! We certainly have tools and machines now to pull it off now;
- Characters are done very well and are in range of an average action thriller, so you'll basically get two-in-one: a decent action thriller and a game. Main character role is a bit shallow, but it's all compensated by Vaas, one of the best portrayed villains in gaming history;
- Weapons!
- In the first part of the game you are skilling up and acquiring new weapons to play with. Many games make a mistake of ending right when you have finished skilling and you only get to use all of what you gained in final boss fight at best. Far Cry 3 does not make that mistake. In fact, the whole second part of the game is made for you to actually play with all the skills and weapons you grinded (take notes, Square Enix, your DeusEx could use some of this magic);
Cons:
EDIT: Please note: While I stand behind cons listed below, the stabilitry of UPlay improved after several updates of it and I don't have problems with it any more... for now...
- Uplay... Do I need to say more? Ok, I will. The game crashed about 20-ish times in 3 days of playing while updating inventory (buying stuff, selling stuff,...) because it failed to sync with Uplay. How can any game studio allow a game as good as this to have severe technical dificulties caused by their DRM software is beyond me. It almost makes you buy the game to get your CD key and then use a pirate copy of it with your original key just to avoid the utter nonsense you are forced to bare when using Uplay. I say "almost" because the autosave is pretty decently implemented so you won't lose much when the game crashes. And ultimatelly, what's wrong with using Steam? Just because anyone can build their own game delivery system, it doesn't mean anyone should. Resource consumption of Uplay and their game launcher is whole another story.
- Default weapon selection menu is obviously ported from consoles or made to be used by game controllers. Usability of it on keyboard+mouse is far from smooth.

